    description: "FOUND 2026-09-18 while investigating whether dropping SCIP local Symbols is\nconsistent with the coexistence design. It could not be answered, because the\ndesign is asserted in three places with three different statuses and the one\ncitation everybody uses does not contain it.\n\n=== 1. THE SPEC ASSERTS IT AS SETTLED CURRENT BEHAVIOUR ===\n\ndocs/hypergumbo-spec.md, in the taint/io-boundary section:\n\n  \"Which Rust backend you select does not affect this: attribute-reference\n   coverage is the tree-sitter analyzer's, the SCIP backend emits no\n   module_attr_ref edges of its own, and **the two run alongside each other\n   rather than one replacing the other (ADR-0012 coexistence; WI-gojum)**\"\n\nAGENTS.md names the spec as the design contract, so this is the sentence a\nreader is entitled to rely on.\n\n=== 2. THE CITED ADR CONTAINS NONE OF IT ===\n\nVerified by term over docs/adr/0012-pass-unification-and-multi-fidelity.md:\n\n    scip            0 occurrences\n    rust-analyzer   0\n    dedup           0\n    multi-fidelity  11\n    coexist          2\n\nBoth \"coexist\" uses are about something else: 0012:10 is about \"two coexisting\nanalyzer REGISTRATION systems\", and 0012:52 is about two EDGES at one callsite\n(\"Both edges coexist: ast_call_method (0.85) and type_resolved_call (0.95)\").\nNeither is about two backends emitting duplicate SYMBOLS. ADR-0012's own status\nline reads \"Step 1 implemented (analyzer registration unified); Steps 2-3 remain\ndesign targets\", and 0012:122 calls Steps 2-3 a \"speculative generalization\nrisk ... with no current user demand\".\n\nSo \"ADR-0012 coexistence\" is a citation to a ruling that was never written\nthere.\n\n=== 3. THE VISION DOC LISTS IT AS AN OPEN QUESTION ===\n\ndocs/future/scip-generalization-vision.md:251-253, under Open Questions:\n\n  \"**Coexistence vs. replacement.** Does the SCIP-backed analyzer for language X\n   run alongside the tree-sitter analyzer (as Rust does now) or replace it?\n   Coexistence preserves the fallback; replacement avoids double-dipping but\n   loses the safety net.\"\n\n=== 4. AND WI-gojum SAYS CHANGING IT NEEDS AN AMENDMENT NOBODY WROTE ===\n\nWI-gojum (parked by the owner 2026-07-19): \"ADR-0012's intended multi-fidelity\ndesign is COEXISTENCE ... NOT 'rust-analyzer replaces tree-sitter.' A replace\ndesign would AMEND ADR-0012.\"\n\n=== WHY THIS IS NOT PEDANTRY ===\n\n(a) It is already propagating into NEW work. WI-nanom -- \"Python multi-fidelity\n    backend: pyright/SCIP receiver typing (ADR-0012 coexistence, spec s20)\" --\n    proposes a SECOND backend on this citation chain, using the Rust arm as\n    shipped precedent.\n\n(b) The question is live and unanswerable as filed. Asked \"is dropping SCIP\n    local Symbols consistent with the design?\", there is no document that both\n    (i) rules on symbol-level duplication and (ii) is a decision document.\n\n(c) The de-facto answer is COEXIST, by default rather than by ruling: nothing in\n    the shipped pipeline merges, dedups, prefers or reconciles the two arms.\n    Symbols are concatenated (all_analyzers.py:273-275, a bare list.extend with\n    no key). Edges ARE deduped but on endpoint Symbol.ids, which differ between\n    arms, so it cannot collapse cross-backend pairs. The cross-pass symbol dedup\n    was never written -- git log -S over the whole history finds no commit that\n    adds, moves or removes one.\n\nWHAT THIS ROW ASKS FOR, AND IT IS THE OWNER'S. Either:\n  1. AMEND ADR-0012 (or write a new ADR) to actually rule on backend\n     coexistence at the SYMBOL level -- what duplication is intended, what a\n     consumer should do with it, and whether dedup is a goal or a non-goal; or\n  2. RETRACT the spec sentence's citation and say plainly that coexistence is\n     current BEHAVIOUR with no decision behind it, pointing at WI-gojum as the\n     open question.\n\nEither is fine. What is not fine is the present state, where a reader following\nthe citation finds nothing and a second backend is being planned on it.\n\nAGENT SCOPE: I can do (2) and the doc repairs. (1) is a decision reserved to the\nowner, and ADR-0056 item 5 is an adjacent reserved question, so this row is filed\nneeds_human_review rather than actioned."  # 9f1a
    fields: {}  # 9f1a
